course: dictionary
lesson: 5010
title: Making inferences
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An inference is an idea you work out from clues, not an idea the writer tells you straight out. «She slammed the door and stamped up the stairs.» The text never says Mia is cross, but the clues show it, so you infer that she is angry.
explain: An inference must fit ALL the clues, not just one. If you look at only one clue, you may guess wrongly. Check every clue: does your idea still fit when you read the whole sentence or passage?
explain: You make inferences in science too. You see wet ground and infer that it rained. In maths and statistics, an inference is a conclusion drawn from data by careful reasoning.
explain: A good inference gives the idea and the evidence. Say what you think, then point to the clue that shows it: «She was angry because she slammed the door.»
fact: An inference is an idea worked out from clues, not one that is stated directly.
fact: Inference means working out something the text suggests but does not say directly.
fact: To make an inference is to work something out from what you already know.
fact: Useful phrases are make an inference, draw an inference and by inference.
fact: A good inference fits all the clues and can be supported by evidence from the text.
exercise: «She slammed the door and stamped up the stairs.» What can you infer about Mia? | She is very tired and wants to sleep | She is lost in the house | *She is angry | She has just won a prize
exercise: Mrs Patel sees wet ground and puddles in the yard. What can she infer? | It snowed last night | The gardener washed the car with a hose | *It rained | The sun melted a lake
exercise: Sam's coat is dripping, his hair is wet and he is shivering. What can you infer? | *He has been out in the rain | He is on holiday in the desert | He has just eaten a big dinner
test: «Ben tapped his pencil and kept checking the clock.» What can you infer? | He has finished all his homework | He is a famous writer | He is fast asleep | *He is worried about the time
test: A bag has 10 sweets and 9 of them are red. What is a fair inference about the next sweet you take? | It will be a toffee | It is impossible to guess anything about it | It is definitely green | *It is probably red
exam: «The windows were wide open, the rug was wet and a vase lay in pieces.» Which inference fits ALL the clues? | The room is brand new and has never been used | *Something or someone caused a mess in a hurry | Nobody has ever been in the room | The rug is perfectly dry and the vase is whole
